    "body": "I feel like that comment is not really true even before this change (sometimes a module produces multiple segments that can't be configured independently, sometimes two different elements that share a style might be combined) and definitely isn't correct after this change (because we're actively splitting segments on newlines). \r\n\r\nThe thing that seems to be notable about a segment is that it's a chunk of text that has the same styling. It is true that for the most part a segment will be a logical chunk of text, but that's more of a byproduct of sensible styling choices than inherent in the type. \r\n\r\neg. we could create a format string like `\"[r](red)[a](yellow)[i](green)[n](cyan)[b](blue)[o](magenta)[w](white)\"` which would be 7 segments but really only one element (a word). This is just uncommon, b/c shell prompts generally use styles for color coding, not just decoration. \r\n\r\nI'm open to expanding on the comment though. How about this?\r\n```\r\n/// A segment is a chunk of text with an associated style.\r\n/// In general, modules should produce one segment for each configurable element in the module. \r\n/// (eg. The version that software is running might be one element, and the symbol for the module a second segment).\r\n```\r\n(Though TBH, to me it feels like the last two lines really belong as a comment on `Module not on Segment)\r\n",
    "created_at": "2021-09-10T20:25:59Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-09-10T20:25:59Z",
